# local/manifest — pinned single-binary tarballs for Linux, installed by
# local/install.sh into ~/.local/bin (no root, no system packages). macOS:
# the Brewfile owns these tools — this file never runs there.
#
# One tool per line, seven pipe-separated fields (greppable, in the spirit
# of the old apt/packages):
#
#   name|version|owner/repo|tag|asset-x86_64|asset-aarch64|min
#
#   name     binary name, both inside the tarball and in ~/.local/bin
#   version  pinned version (informational; also the refresh threshold)
#   repo     GitHub owner/name; download URL is
#            .../releases/download/<tag>/<asset>
#   tag      release tag exactly as published (the v-prefix varies per
#            project — that is why it is a separate field)
#   asset-*  exact asset file name per architecture, written out in full:
#            release naming is too heterogeneous to template safely
#   min      oldest SYSTEM copy accepted instead of installing (see the
#            ladder in local/lib.sh); "-" = always install our own
#
# Tuned so Ubuntu 22.04/24.04 and RHEL 9 machines keep acceptable system
# copies and only download what their distro lacks (or ships too old).
# Archive shapes handled: *.tar.gz, *.tar.xz, *.zip, and raw files (jq).

